<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wends</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wends is a historical name used by Germans and others for Slavic peoples living near Germanic lands, especially between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and the Oder. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar">Chronicle of Fredegar</a> applies it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samo</a> in the 7th century, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it chiefly meant the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polabian-slavs">Polabian Slavs</a>, conquered and converted by German princes in the 12th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorbs">Sorbs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> are still sometimes called Wends.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wends.jpg" alt="Wends" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Limes.saxoniae.wmt.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
